Next-token prediction (simple explanation)

What is Next-Token Prediction?
Next-token prediction is a fundamental task in natural language processing (NLP) and AI language models. It means predicting the very next word or token in a sequence of text, given all the previous words or tokens.

Token: A token can be a word, part of a word, or even a single character, depending on the model's tokenizer.
Goal: Given a sentence fragment like "The cat sat on the", the model predicts the next token, e.g., "mat".
How Does It Work?
Input: A sequence of tokens (words or subwords).
Model: A trained language model (like GPT, Transformer-based models) processes the input.
Output: The model outputs a probability distribution over the entire vocabulary for the next token.
Prediction: The token with the highest probability is chosen as the next token.
Step-by-Step Example
Suppose the input is:

"I love to eat"
The model looks at these tokens.
It calculates the probability of every possible next token.
For example:
"pizza" → 0.4
"apples" → 0.3
"books" → 0.1
...others with lower probabilities
The model predicts "pizza" as the next token because it has the highest probability.
Why is Next-Token Prediction Important?
It is the core task behind many AI language models.
Enables text generation, autocomplete, translation, and chatbots.
Helps models learn grammar, context, and semantics by training on large text corpora.
Visual Diagram
Input Tokens:  [I] [love] [to] [eat]
                      ↓
Model predicts next token probabilities:
  pizza (0.4), apples (0.3), books (0.1), ...
                      ↓
Next token predicted: [pizza]
Real-World Example
When you type in your phone's keyboard, it suggests the next word based on what you typed so far — this is next-token prediction in action.
AI chatbots generate responses by repeatedly predicting the next token until the sentence is complete.
